-- title:
--   Condition 35 — norm-subGaussian martingale difference sequences (with Definition 32)
-- statement:
--   A random vector $X\in\mathbb R^d$ is **norm-subGaussian** with parameter $\sigma$, written $\mathrm{nSG}(\sigma)$, if $\mathbb P(\|X-\mathbb EX\|\ge t)\le 2e^{-t^2/(2\sigma^2)}$ for all $t\ge0$ (Definition 32).
--
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathbb P)$ be a probability space with a filtration $(\mathcal F_i)_{i\ge0}$. Random vectors $X_1,\dots,X_n\in\mathbb R^d$ with random scale parameters $\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_n$ satisfy **Condition 35** if, for every $i\in[n]=\{1,\dots,n\}$, $X_i$ is $\mathcal F_i$-measurable and integrable, $\sigma_i>0$ is $\mathcal F_{i-1}$-measurable, and $X_i$ given $\mathcal F_{i-1}$ is zero-mean $\mathrm{nSG}(\sigma_i)$:
--   $$\mathbb E[X_i\mid\mathcal F_{i-1}]=0,\qquad \mathbb P\big(\|X_i\|\ge t\mid\mathcal F_{i-1}\big)\le 2e^{-t^2/(2\sigma_i^2)}\quad\text{a.s., for all } t\ge0.$$
--
--   This is the norm-subGaussian analogue of a martingale difference sequence with subGaussian increments. The concentration inequalities of Appendix C (Lemmas 36–39) are stated for such sequences, and Appendix B applies them to the stochastic-gradient noise and to the Gaussian perturbations of PSGD.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper uses the natural filtration $\mathcal F_i=\sigma(X_1,\dots,X_i)$; any filtration to which the $X_i$ are adapted is allowed here, since Appendix B applies the lemmas with the larger filtration generated by the noise and the perturbations. The conditional probability is the conditional expectation of the indicator of $\{\|X_i\|\ge t\}$. The bound is required for $t\ge0$ (the printed "$\forall t\in\mathbb R$" is unsatisfiable), and $\sigma_i>0$ because at $\sigma_i=0$ Lean's division by zero would make the bound vacuous. Indices are $1$-based as on the page.
-- source:
--   Jin, Netrapalli, Ge, Kakade, Jordan, On Nonconvex Optimization for Machine Learning: Gradients, Stochasticity, and Saddle Points, arXiv:1902.04811v2, p. 30, Definition 32 and Condition 35

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_NonconvexSaddle_PSGD_Setting

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace NonconvexSaddle.PSGD

/-- Condition 35 (Jin–Netrapalli–Ge–Kakade–Jordan, arXiv:1902.04811v2, App. C, p. 30), the
norm-subGaussian martingale difference condition, with Definition 32 (p. 30) built in. On a
probability space `(Ω, μ)` with a filtration `ℱ`, the random vectors `X₁, …, Xₙ ∈ ℝ^d` (indices
`i ∈ [n] = {1, …, n}`, as on the page) and the scale parameters `σ_i` satisfy, for every `i ∈ [n]`:
* `X_i` is `ℱ_i`-measurable and integrable, and `σ_i` is `ℱ_{i−1}`-measurable with `σ_i > 0`;
* `E[X_i | ℱ_{i−1}] = 0`;
* `P(‖X_i‖ ≥ t | ℱ_{i−1}) ≤ 2 exp(−t²/(2σ_i²))` almost surely, for every `t ≥ 0`.

The page takes the natural filtration `ℱ_i = σ(X₁, …, X_i)`; any filtration to which `X` is
adapted is allowed here (the natural one is an instance, and App. B applies the lemmas with the
larger filtration `𝓕_t = σ(ζ₀, ξ₀, …, ζ_t, ξ_t)`). The page's "`∀ t ∈ ℝ`" is unsatisfiable for
`t < 0`, so `t ≥ 0`; `σ_i > 0` because at `σ_i = 0` Lean's `t²/0 = 0` makes the bound vacuous. -/
structure Condition35 {d : ℕ} {Ω : Type} [m : MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω)
    (ℱ : Filtration ℕ m) (n : ℕ) (X : ℕ → Ω → E d) (σ : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) : Prop where
  adapted : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, StronglyMeasurable[ℱ i] (X i)
  integrable : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, Integrable (X i) μ
  sigma_measurable : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, Measurable[ℱ (i - 1)] (σ i)
  sigma_pos : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, ∀ ω, 0 < σ i ω
  zero_mean : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, μ[X i | ℱ (i - 1)] =ᵐ[μ] 0
  tail : ∀ i ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t →
    μ[{ω | t ≤ ‖X i ω‖}.indicator (fun _ => (1 : ℝ)) | ℱ (i - 1)]
      ≤ᵐ[μ] fun ω => 2 * Real.exp (-t ^ 2 / (2 * σ i ω ^ 2))

end NonconvexSaddle.PSGD


